course: dictionary
lesson: 12907
title: Erroneously and other formal words
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Erroneously» is an adverb. An adverb tells you how something was done, thought or said. This one is a formal word, so you will mostly meet it in news reports, letters and exam texts.
explain: «The newspaper erroneously printed the wrong date.» The clues are «printed the wrong date»: the paper got something wrong, so erroneously must mean by mistake.
explain: To check, swap in an everyday word: «The newspaper wrongly printed the wrong date.» It still makes sense, so wrongly is a good match. Erroneously means wrongly, or by mistake.
fact: «Erroneously» is an adverb, so it tells how something happened.
fact: «Erroneously» means wrongly or by mistake.
fact: The root «error» means mistake, which is a helpful clue.
fact: Formal writers use «erroneously»; everyday speech would say «by mistake» or «wrongly».
fact: A good check is to swap in «wrongly» or «by mistake» and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «Mia erroneously wrote 7 instead of 9 on her sum.» What does erroneously mean? | On purpose | Very quickly | *By mistake | With great care
exercise: Which everyday words mean the same as erroneously? | Gladly | *Wrongly | Proudly | Slowly
exercise: «The team ___ believed the match was cancelled.» Which word fits the gap? | Ancient | Glum | Curt | *Erroneously
test: «He erroneously sent the letter to the wrong address.» What does erroneously tell us? | He was very happy about it | He sent it very fast | *He made a mistake | He did it on purpose
test: «The news erroneously reported that the school was shut.» Which swap keeps the meaning? | Slowly | *Wrongly | Angrily | Happily
exam: Which word is closest in meaning to erroneously? | Quickly | Gladly | Quietly | *Wrongly
